course: dictionary
lesson: 5855
title: Mam, mum and ma'am
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change depending on where a person grows up. In parts of Wales, the north of England and Ireland, many children say «mam» for their mother. «My mam» means my mum, so the meaning is the same, but the word sounds different, and that tells you something about where the family lives.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «In Swansea, Saffie's mam works two jobs, so Saffie gets home at quarter to four.» Saffie's mam is at work, and Saffie goes home to an empty house, so mam must be a parent. The place name Swansea is another clue that this is a Welsh word.
explain: Mam can also be a spelling of «ma'am», a polite way to speak to a woman, especially in the USA. «Yes, ma'am» is said to a woman or a teacher. The two words sound alike, so always check the sentence: does it talk about a family, or about being polite? Try «mum» in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: Mam is an informal word for mother, used in Wales, the north of England and Ireland.
fact: «Mam» and «mum» mean the same thing; «mam» is simply the word many families use in those places.
fact: «Ma'am» is a polite way to speak to a woman, especially in the USA, as in «yes, ma'am».
fact: Mam can also be a spelling of «ma'am» (madam), so use the sentence to decide which meaning fits.
fact: «Me mam» is dialect, which means it is the local way some people speak.
exercise: «Saffie's mam works two jobs.» In this sentence, what does mam mean? | A school bell | *Mother | A polite way to speak to a woman in the USA | A kind neighbour
exercise: «Yes, ma'am.» What does ma'am mean here? | A type of bread | Grandad | Mother | *A polite way to speak to a woman
exercise: Elsie's aunt says gently, «Your mam loves you.» Who is mam? | Elsie's aunt | Elsie's neighbour | *Elsie's mother | Elsie's teacher
test: Callie's mam says, «Ye're fine, stop greetin'». What does greetin' mean? | Sleeping | Laughing | Running | *Crying
test: Which word means mum in parts of Wales and the north of England? | *Mam | Lush | Cheers | Tip
exam: In «a mam in prison», what does mam mean? | A teacher | *A mother | A dad | A grandmother
